  "range-hood-noisy-windy-days": {
    title: "Why Is Your Range Hood Noisy on Windy Days? Causes, Fixes & Expert Tips",
    cat: "Range Hoods", date: "November 15, 2025", read: "6 min read",
    img: "assets/cat-hood.webp",
    tags: ["Range Hoods","Troubleshooting","Tips"],
    body: `
      <p class="lead">If your range hood suddenly rattles, whistles or bangs on windy days, you're not imagining it — and it's usually fixable. Here's what's happening inside the ducting, what the different noises mean, and how to quiet it down.</p>
      <h2><span class="no">01</span>Why Wind Makes Range Hoods Noisy</h2>
      <p>Strong gusts push air backward through the ductwork, shaking internal components. Exposed rooftop vents, aging or loose duct seals, and basic gravity dampers that swing freely all make the problem worse — the wind essentially fights against your hood's airflow.</p>
      <h2><span class="no">02</span>What the Different Noises Mean</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Flapping or banging</b> — a loose backdraft damper or exterior vent flap swinging in the wind.</li>
        <li><b>Whistling or howling</b> — air forcing through gaps, cracks or misaligned ducts from poor sealing.</li>
        <li><b>Rattling</b> — loose screws, unsteady duct joints or worn fittings vibrating.</li>
        <li><b>Sudden thumps</b> — a strong gust slamming the damper shut, or a sticking damper snapping back.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="pull">A noisy hood on a windy day is almost always the ducting talking — and it's usually a simple fix.</div>
      <h2><span class="no">03</span>What to Inspect First</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The <b>exterior vent cap</b> for rust, dents or loose seals.</li>
        <li>The <b>backdraft damper</b> — is it aligned and moving freely?</li>
        <li><b>Duct joints</b> for gaps and missing insulation.</li>
        <li>The <b>fan assembly</b> for any vibration or looseness.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2><span class="no">04</span>How to Fix a Noisy Range Hood</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Tighten loose screws</b> throughout the assembly.</li>
        <li><b>Seal duct joints</b> with weatherproof sealant.</li>
        <li><b>Fit a wind-resistant or louvered vent cap</b> outside.</li>
        <li><b>Upgrade to a spring-loaded backdraft damper</b> that resists gusts.</li>
        <li><b>Improve duct insulation</b> to dampen vibration and noise.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2><span class="no">05</span>When to Replace the System</h2>
      <p>If the noise persists after these fixes — or you notice rusted parts, weak airflow, or a system that's simply 10–15 years old — it may be time to replace it. A modern, well-sealed hood with a quality damper runs quietly even when the weather doesn't cooperate.</p>
      <div class="callout"><h4>Built quiet by design</h4><p>Fotile range hoods are engineered for quiet, stable airflow with quality dampers and sealed ducting paths — so powerful ventilation doesn't come with the rattle.</p></div>
    `
  },

